{"status":"success","paper_skeleton":{"abstract":"本文提出3DGStream，一种面向真实动态场景的高效自由视点视频流构建方法。该方法使用3D高斯表示场景，并采用神经变换缓存(NTC)建模3D高斯的平移与旋转，大幅减少每帧训练时间和存储；同时提出自适应3D高斯添加策略处理动态场景中的新出现物体。实验表明，3DGStream在渲染速度、图像质量、训练时间和模型存储方面均具竞争力，可实现约12秒的即时逐帧重建和200 FPS实时渲染。","introduction":"从多视角视频构建自由视点视频仍具挑战。传统几何和图像方法难以处理复杂真实场景；NeRF类方法通常需要完整视频进行离线训练且难以实时渲染。受3D高斯泼溅(3DGS)实时渲染启发，本文提出3DGStream：先训练初始帧3D高斯，随后逐帧以上一帧3D高斯为初始化，结合神经变换缓存和自适应添加策略实现在线重建和实时渲染。","methods":"3DGStream采用逐帧在线训练框架：在时刻0训练初始3D高斯；后续每一时刻以上一帧3D高斯初始化，并经过两阶段处理。第一阶段训练神经变换缓存(NTC)建模每个3D高斯的变换，训练后对3D高斯进行变换并传递到下一时刻；第二阶段采用自适应3D高斯添加策略处理新出现物体。当前帧渲染使用变换后的3D高斯和新增3D高斯，新增3D高斯不传入下一帧。每帧只需训练和存储NTC与新增3D高斯的参数。实现基于3DGS代码与tiny-cuda-nn，NTC训练150次迭代，新增3D高斯训练100次迭代。","results":"实验表明，NTC在建模3D高斯变换时：去除多分辨率哈希编码会明显降低建模能力，去除预热会减慢收敛；与直接优化上一帧3D高斯相比，NTC达到相近性能且存储显著降低。自适应3D高斯添加策略可有效重建初帧中不存在的物体；无第二阶段或随机生成高斯的变体无法捕获新出现物体，去除数量控制则会产生过多新增高斯。此外，NTC查询和多分辨率哈希编码及全融合MLP效率较高，SH旋转是主要额外耗时步骤但影响很小。","discussion":"方法质量依赖初始帧3DGS质量，因此继承了3DGS的局限，如对初始点云依赖较强；在远景区域因COLMAP难以重建可能出现伪影。此外，为满足在线训练效率而限制训练迭代次数，导致对剧烈运动和复杂新出现物体的建模能力受限。","conclusion":"本文提出3DGStream，一种高效的在线自由视点视频流方法。其基于3DGS，利用神经变换缓存捕捉物体运动，并采用自适应3D高斯添加策略建模动态场景中的新出现物体。两阶段管线支持视频流在线重建，在保证照片级图像质量的同时，实现约10秒/帧的在线训练、约200 FPS的百万像素实时渲染以及适中存储开销。"},"paper_structure":{"research_problem":"Constructing photo-realistic Free-Viewpoint Videos of dynamic scenes from multi-view videos is challenging; current neural rendering methods usually require complete video sequences for offline training and cannot achieve real-time rendering.","core_hypothesis":"Dynamic scenes can be represented with 3D Gaussians and streamed efficiently by using a compact Neural Transformation Cache to model per-frame transformations and an adaptive 3D Gaussian addition strategy to handle emerging objects, enabling fast on-the-fly reconstruction and real-time rendering.","method_framework":["Train initial 3D Gaussians on multi-view frames at timestep 0.","For each subsequent timestep, initialize with the previous timestep's 3D Gaussians.","Stage 1: Train a Neural Transformation Cache to model translations and rotations of the 3D Gaussians, then transform and carry them to the next timestep.","Stage 2: Apply an adaptive 3D Gaussian addition strategy to model emerging objects in the dynamic scene.","Render the current timestep using transformed 3D Gaussians plus additional 3D Gaussians, while only the NTC parameters and additional 3D Gaussians are stored per frame."],"claimed_contributions":["Proposes 3DGStream, a method for efficient Free-Viewpoint Video streaming of dynamic scenes using 3D Gaussians.","Introduces a Neural Transformation Cache to model 3D Gaussian transformations, substantially reducing per-frame training time and storage.","Proposes an adaptive 3D Gaussian addition strategy to handle emerging objects.","Achieves fast on-the-fly training and real-time rendering with competitive image quality, training time, and storage compared to state-of-the-art methods."],"experimental_logic":"The method is evaluated on the N3DV and Meet Room datasets. Initial 3D Gaussians are trained at timestep 0, then each subsequent frame uses the previous frame's 3D Gaussians. Ablations on the flame salmon scene compare the Neural Transformation Cache against direct optimization and variants without multi-resolution hash encoding or warm-up, showing that NTC approaches direct optimization quality with substantially lower storage. Adaptive 3D Gaussian addition is ablated against no Stage 2, random spawning, and no quantity control, demonstrating that it reconstructs emerging objects with minimal added 3D Gaussians. Rendering speed is measured using the SIBR framework, and overhead is analyzed in terms of NTC query and SH rotation.","limitations":"3DGStream inherits limitations of 3D Gaussian Splatting, including high dependence on the initial point cloud, which can cause artifacts in regions such as distant landscapes that COLMAP fails to reconstruct. The limited number of training iterations restricts modeling of drastic motion in Stage 1 and complex emerging objects in Stage 2.","outlineVersion":"1.4","sections":[{"id":"outline-d7ca0013cc","title":"Abstract","displayTitle":"Abstract","rawTitle":"Abstract","headingNumber":"","level":1,"nestedLevel":1,"parentId":null,"pageIndex":0,"page":1,"bbox":{"x":146.0,"y":255.61,"width":43.04,"height":14.35},"anchorY":255.61299999999994,"source":"pdf-layout","sourceDetails":["pdf-line-heading"],"confidence":0.78,"preview":"","type":"section","order":1},{"id":"outline-1-2","title":"1 Introduction","displayTitle":"1 Introduction","rawTitle":"Introduction","headingNumber":"1","level":1,"nestedLevel":1,"parentId":null,"pageIndex":0,"page":1,"bbox":{"x":50.11,"y":545.13,"width":76.84,"height":10.75},"anchorY":545.13,"source":"tei","sourceDetails":["tei-head"],"confidence":0.95,"preview":"Constructing Free-Viewpoint Videos (FVVs) from videos captured by a set of known-poses cameras from multiple views remains a frontier challenge within the domains of computer visio","type":"section","order":2},{"id":"outline-2-3","title":"2 Related Work","displayTitle":"2 Related Work","rawTitle":"Related Work","headingNumber":"2","level":1,"nestedLevel":1,"parentId":null,"pageIndex":1,"page":2,"bbox":{"x":308.86,"y":281.13,"width":83.11,"height":10.75},"anchorY":281.13,"source":"tei+layout","sourceDetails":["pdf-line-heading","tei-head"],"confidence":0.95,"preview":"","type":"section","order":3},{"id":"outline-2-1-4","title":"2.1 Novel View Synthesis for Static Scenes","displayTitle":"2.1 Novel View Synthesis for Static Scenes","rawTitle":"Novel View Synthesis for Static Scenes","headingNumber":"2.1","level":2,"nestedLevel":2,"parentId":"outline-2-3","pageIndex":1,"page":2,"bbox":{"x":308.86,"y":300.78,"width":198.17,"height":9.85},"anchorY":300.78,"source":"tei","sourceDetails":["tei-head"],"confidence":0.95,"preview":"Synthesizing novel views from a set of images of static scenes is a time-honored problem in the domains of computer vision and graphics. Traditional methods such as Lumigraph [8, 2","type":"section","order":4},{"id":"outline-2-2-5","title":"2.2 Free-Viewpoint Videos of Dynamic Scenes","displayTitle":"2.2 Free-Viewpoint Videos of Dynamic Scenes","rawTitle":"Free-Viewpoint Videos of Dynamic Scenes","headingNumber":"2.2","level":2,"nestedLevel":2,"parentId":"outline-2-3","pageIndex":1,"page":2,"bbox":{"x":308.86,"y":637.65,"width":216.01,"height":9.85},"anchorY":637.65,"source":"tei+layout","sourceDetails":["pdf-line-heading","tei-head"],"confidence":0.95,"preview":"Constructing FVVs from a set of videos of dynamic scenes is a more challenging and applicable task in the domains of computer vision and graphics. Earlier attempts to address this ","type":"section","order":5},{"id":"outline-2-3-6","title":"2.3 Concurrent Works","displayTitle":"2.3 Concurrent Works","rawTitle":"Concurrent Works","headingNumber":"2.3","level":2,"nestedLevel":2,"parentId":"outline-2-3","pageIndex":2,"page":3,"bbox":{"x":308.86,"y":672.07,"width":107.34,"height":9.85},"anchorY":672.07,"source":"tei","sourceDetails":["tei-head"],"confidence":0.95,"preview":"Except for Dynamic3DG, several concurrent works have extended 3DG-S to represent dynamic scenes. De-formable3DG [71] employs an MLP to model the deformation of 3DGs, while [66] int","type":"section","order":6},{"id":"outline-3-7","title":"3 Background: 3D Gaussian Splatting","displayTitle":"3 Background: 3D Gaussian Splatting","rawTitle":"Background: 3D Gaussian Splatting","headingNumber":"3","level":1,"nestedLevel":1,"parentId":null,"pageIndex":3,"page":4,"bbox":{"x":50.11,"y":174.48,"width":196.81,"height":10.75},"anchorY":174.48,"source":"tei+layout","sourceDetails":["pdf-line-heading","tei-head"],"confidence":0.95,"preview":"3D Gaussian Splatting (3DG-S) [26] employs anisotropic 3D Gaussians as an explicit scene representation. Paired with a fast differentiable rasterizer, 3DGs achieves real-time novel","type":"section","order":7},{"id":"outline-3-1-8","title":"3.1 3D Gaussians as Scene Representation","displayTitle"